                        <h5><a href="blog-post.html">Why Learning an African Language is So Important</a></h5>
                        <div class="info">
                            POSTED BY: <a href="#">Dr Nike. Lawal</a> <span>|</span> COMMENTS: <a href="#">Not Allowed</a>
                            <span>|</span> DATE: <a href="#">August 27, 2015</a>
                        </div>
                        <div class="border">
                            <img class="scale-with-grid" src="~/Images/blog-train.jpg" />
                            <a class='link' href="blog-post.html"></a>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Ten years ago, China, was the next big thing in the global economy and the move to learn Chinese and make inroads within that country happened at a frenzied pace. However, now as we move forward in this new millennial, the face of the global economy and world is going to be changing even more rapidly within the next decade and a lot of that will be due in part to the role African countries, who are poised to become major players in the market like China was a decade ago will play. With the Eye Report survey of 2011, which stated that 43% of the 562 business decision makers and investors across the world were in favor of investing in Africa, it is predicted that over the next five years, the average African economies will outpace its Asian counterpart. This means that in the next twenty years, learning an African language is going to be one of the most important skills to  acquire.
                        </p>

                        <p class='extra'>
                            While many countries on the continent are part of this new global economic front, countries in the West African region are going to play a big part, and of the West African countries poised to propel the African continent as a major player in this new global economy is Nigeria, also known as the giant of Africa. After the re-basement of its economy in 2014, Nigeria became the largest economy in sub-Saharan Africa with a GDP of $504 billion dollars, significantly surpassing that of South Africa which has a GDP of $302 billion. Nigeria is known as the giant of Africa not only because of its large economy but also because of its population. With a population of 178, 516, 905, as of July 1, 2014, Nigeria is ranked the seventh on the list of largest population in the world.  It is therefore an attractive market for investors and companies. Many American companies have major business operations in Nigeria. However, other countries are also getting involved in Nigeria, among which are China and India.
                        </p>
                        <div class="foot">
                            <p class="left"><strong>CATEGORY: <a href="#">Website</a></strong></p>
                            <p class="right"><a href="blog-post.html">Read More</a></p>
                            <div class="clear"></div>
                        </div>
                    </div><!-- Blog Ends -->

                    <div class="blog">
                        <h5><a href="blog-post.html">Our Diversity</a></h5>
                        <div class="info">
                            POSTED BY: <a href="#">SupremeGeneral</a> <span>|</span> COMMENTS: <a href="#">2 Comments</a>
                            <span>|</span> DATE: <a href="#">September 21, 2012</a>
                        </div>
                        <div class="border shadow">
                            <img class="scale-with-grid" src="~/Content/images/adults class.jpg" />
                            <a class='link' href="blog-post.html"></a>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Africa, is making an impact on the global market not only as a place to invest in the establishment of businesses and building of  infrastructures such as roads, bridges and housing, factories, it can also be found in extends also to movies, music, and the fashion industry. For instance, the Nigerian movie industry known as Nollywood is the biggest movie industry on the continent and ranks third in the world behind Hollywood and Bollywood. On the music front, some of the international music awards held in the United States the past few years have been dominated by Nigerian and Ghanaian artistes and many American R&B singers regularly go to Nigeria to look for talent and to collaborate with Nigerian artists such as Wiz kid. 
                        </p>

                          <p>
                              Investors and companies are constantly looking for new emerging markets to trade with and invest in. Learning an African language is therefore a smart thing to do as it gives one an opportunity to be involved the new emerging markets of Africa. In a world which is becoming increasingly global, knowledge of an African language gives one an insider’s view into another continent and an ability to communicate and understand another culture, an asset which cannot be overlooked in this new economic world order. 

                          </p>
